        "text": "WHAT THIS FACT EXISTS TO SHOW. Its statement is the binary32 instance of its binary16 sibling, and it is settled TWICE by routes of different character: symbolically, by bit-blasting a miter of the two circuits to CNF and refuting it (which says nothing about any particular input and everything about all of them at once), and by brute force, enumerating every one of the 4294967296 encodings. The second is possible here only because the claim is UNARY. The ternary claims in this batch -- F:fp8-add-not-associative and F:fp8-add-monotone-rne -- are exhaustive at fp8 E5M2 (2^24 triples) and would need 2^48 triples at binary16 and 2^96 at binary32, so for those the honest scope really is \"exhaustive at 8 bits, symbolic above\". Arity, not width, is what decides whether brute force is available.\n\nCOST, MEASURED. Symbolic: axeyum 202s, z3 0.1s, bitwuzla 0.1s. Exhaustive: 51s wall / 361s CPU. The symbolic route is the one that would still work at binary64; the exhaustive one would not.\n\nEVIDENCE ORDER IS DELIBERATE. The exhaustive row is first because it is the row that carries the runnable checker and rests on the least: it does not go through axeyum's fpa2bv lowering, whose `add` and `mul` are documented as validated rather than proven, and which the second row's footprint has to assume.",
        "type": "prose"
      },
